2026-05-07 21:13:21,775 subagents.hld_v_agent.agent INFO hld_v[important] triggers[0] — The external-event trigger has no edge case covering duplicate webhook delivery before any signups have been notified — the idempotency text on the trigger itself addresses the 'all already notified' case, but there is no edgeCase entry handling a scenario where the same restock webhook fires twice concurrently while notifications are still in flight. If two webhook deliveries race, `identify-pending-signups` will return the same pending rows for both, and `send-restock-notification` will fire twice for every customer. The edgeCases list covers only the post-hoc duplicate (edge case 2), not the concurrent in-flight race.
2026-05-07 21:13:21,776 subagents.hld_v_agent.agent INFO hld_v[important] capabilities[2] (cancel-signup) — cancel-signup has usesWorkflow=true, but register-signup — the capability that creates the initial pending row — has usesWorkflow=false. The HLD spec correctly sets usesWorkflow=false on initial-state inserts, but cancel-signup is a single terminal-state writer for the 'unsubscribed' branch, while record-notification-outcome (usesWorkflow=true) is the writer for the 'notified' and 'failed' terminal states. These two write capabilities are triggered by completely different actors (customer-initiated vs. system-automated) and write to the same status column on product_signups, which is correct decomposition — but cancel-signup lists only 'customer email' and 'variant external id' as dataNeeds, with no signup id. Without the row identifier in dataNeeds, the LLD has no anchor to locate and lock the specific row being transitioned, creating a gap where a concurrent notify could race against an unsubscribe on the same record.
2026-05-07 21:13:21,776 subagents.hld_v_agent.agent INFO hld_v[important] externalContracts[0] (GET /widget/signup) — The widget GET requestShape includes 'customer_email' as a caller-supplied field. On a storefront widget, the page context does not reliably expose the customer's email address — it must be discovered from the session/cookie or typed by the user, neither of which the widget can supply as a pre-known value on page load. Requiring email in the GET request means the widget cannot render the correct initial state without prior user input, which defeats the purpose of the endpoint (rendering before form submission).
2026-05-07 21:13:21,776 subagents.hld_v_agent.agent INFO hld_v[important] capabilities[5] (send-restock-notification) / edgeCases — There is no edge case describing what happens when the email provider is temporarily unavailable (timeout, 5xx, network error) as distinct from a hard rejection (bounce, invalid address). Edge case 3 covers hard rejection with no retry, but a transient provider failure has a completely different handling requirement — it may warrant a retry or at minimum a different failure_reason category. Without this distinction the LLD will conflate transient and permanent failures, potentially discarding retryable notifications as permanently failed.
2026-05-07 21:13:21,776 subagents.hld_v_agent.agent INFO hld_v[minor] persistence / product_signups — The product_signups table has no column for storing the failure_reason when a signup is marked failed, yet record-notification-outcome writes a 'failure reason' and the notification_attempts table stores it there. If the admin list-signups capability surfaces failure reason inline (which a 'scannable at a glance' dashboard implies), every display of that reason requires a join to notification_attempts. This is not fatal but the dataFlow description says the merchant views 'notification outcomes' from the admin panel — the LLD will need to decide whether to join or denormalise with no guidance from the plan.
